1. Big Picture (One-Line Summary)

JDK → tools to develop Java programs
JRE → environment to run Java programs
JVM → engine that executes Java bytecode

2. Java Architecture Overview

Java Source Code (.java)
        ↓  (javac)
Bytecode (.class)
        ↓
       JVM
   ┌───────────┐
   │ ClassLoader│
   │ Runtime    │
   │ JIT        │
   │ GC         │
   └───────────┘
        ↓
   OS / Hardware

3. JDK (Java Development Kit)
What is JDK?

The JDK is a software development kit used to develop, compile, debug, and run Java applications.

JDK Contains:
JRE
Compiler (javac)
Debugger (jdb)
Archiver (jar)
Documentation tool (javadoc)
Monitoring tools (jconsole, jvisualvm)

Example
javac Hello.java
java Hello

Interview One-liner
“JDK is required to develop Java applications.”

4. JRE (Java Runtime Environment)
What is JRE?
The JRE provides the runtime environment required to run Java applications.

JRE Contains:
JVM
Core Java libraries (java.lang, java.util, etc.)

❌ Does not contain compiler
❌ Cannot develop Java code

Interview One-liner
“JRE is used to run Java applications, not to develop them.”

5. JVM (Java Virtual Machine) – 🔥 Most Important
What is JVM?

The JVM is a virtual machine that:
Loads bytecode
Verifies bytecode
Executes bytecode
Manages memory & garbage collection

Key Feature:
✔ Platform independent execution
Java is compiled to bytecode (platform-independent)
JVM is platform-dependent

6. JVM Internal Architecture (🔥 Must Know)
  6.1 Class Loader Subsystem
  Loads .class files into memory.
  
  Types of Class Loaders:-
  Bootstrap ClassLoader:
  Loads core classes (java.lang)
  
  Extension ClassLoader:
  Loads extension libraries
  
  Application ClassLoader:
  Loads application classes

  6.2 Runtime Data Areas
  1️⃣ Method Area (MetaSpace)
  Class metadata
  Static variables
  Method bytecode
  
  2️⃣ Heap
  Objects & instance variables
  Shared across threads
  
  3️⃣ Stack (per thread)
  Method calls
  Local variables
  Frames
  
  4️⃣ PC Register
  Current executing instruction
  
  5️⃣ Native Method Stack
  For native (C/C++) calls

  6.3 Execution Engine
  Responsible for executing bytecode.
  
  Components:
  Interpreter
  JIT Compiler
  Garbage Collector

7. Interpreter vs JIT Compiler (🔥 Interview Favorite)
| Interpreter            | JIT Compiler                     |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Line-by-line execution | Compiles bytecode to native code |
| Slower                 | Faster                           |
| No optimization        | Highly optimized                 |
| Default initially      | Used for hot code                |
✔ JVM uses both

8. Garbage Collector (GC)
Role:
Automatically removes unused objects
Prevents memory leaks

Key Points:
Works on heap
Uses reachability analysis
Types: Serial, Parallel, G1, ZGC (advanced)

Interview One-liner
“GC automatically manages heap memory.”

9. Java Program Execution Flow (🔥)
Write Java code (.java)
Compile → bytecode (.class)
ClassLoader loads class
Bytecode verifier checks safety
Execution engine executes
Output produced

10. JDK vs JRE vs JVM (🔥 Very Common Table)
| Feature            | JDK     | JRE | JVM     |
| ------------------ | ------- | --- | ------- |
| Purpose            | Develop | Run | Execute |
| Contains JVM       | ✅       | ✅   | —       |
| Contains compiler  | ✅       | ❌   | ❌       |
| Platform dependent | ❌       | ❌   | ✅       |
| Used by developer  | ✅       | ❌   | ❌       |

11. Why Java is Platform Independent?

✔ Bytecode is platform independent
✔ JVM converts bytecode to native code

“Write Once, Run Anywhere”

12. JVM Languages (Advanced)
JVM supports multiple languages:
Java
Kotlin
Scala
Groovy

All compile to bytecode.

Q1. Is JVM platform independent?
❌ No (JVM is platform dependent)

Q2. Is Java fully platform independent?
✅ Yes (because of bytecode + JVM)

Q3. Can we run Java program without JRE?
❌ No

Q4. Can we compile without JDK?
❌ No

